<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fyodor Dostoevsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Dostoevsky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fyodor Dostoevsky was a Russian novelist of the nineteenth century, author of Crime and Punishment and The Brothers Karamazov. He is cited as part of the literary and musical flowering of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Tolstoy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fyodor-dostoevsky.jpg" alt="Fyodor Dostoevsky" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vasily Perov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vasily_Perov_-_%D0%9F%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%A4.%D0%9C.%D0%94%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russian cultural influence spread through the Orthodox Church, the Russian language imposed on subject peoples from Poles to Kazakhs, and the literature and music of the nineteenth century, represented by Pushkin, Tolstoy, <b>Dostoevsky</b>, Tchaikovsky and Mussorgsky.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mariinsky Hospital for the Poor, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.78,37.61&amp;year=1860" aria-label="Mariinsky Hospital for the Poor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 262 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Fyodor Dostoevsky"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 532h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 532h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.78,37.61&amp;year=1860">What was here in 1860? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The man who lies to himself and listens to his own lie comes to such a pass that he cannot distinguish the truth within him, or around him, and so loses all respect for himself and for others.&#8221;</p><footer>The Brothers Karamazov (1880), book 2 &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1912 &#183; spoken by the elder Zosima &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28054/pg28054-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Fathers and teachers, I ponder, “What is hell?” I maintain that it is the suffering of being unable to love.&#8221;</p><footer>The Brothers Karamazov (1880), book 6 &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1912 &#183; the elder Zosima's teaching, as Alyosha wrote it down &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28054/pg28054-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Pain and suffering are always inevitable for a large intelligence and a deep heart.&#8221;</p><footer>Crime and Punishment (1866), part 3 &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1914 &#183; spoken by Raskolnikov &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2554/pg2554-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am a sick man.... I am a spiteful man. I am an unattractive man.&#8221;</p><footer>Notes from Underground (1864), opening &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1918 &#183; spoken by the narrator, the Underground Man &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/600/pg600-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I tell Thee that man is tormented by no greater anxiety than to find some one quickly to whom he can hand over that gift of freedom with which the ill-fated creature is born.&#8221;</p><footer>The Brothers Karamazov (1880), book 5, The Grand Inquisitor &#183; tr. Constance Garnett, 1912 &#183; spoken by the Grand Inquisitor in Ivan Karamazov's poem &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28054/pg28054-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Is it true, prince, that you once declared that ‘beauty would save the world’?&#8221;</p><footer>The Idiot (1869), part 3 &#183; tr. Eva Martin, 1915 &#183; spoken by Ippolit to Prince Myshkin &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2638/pg2638-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
